---
id: org.dxos.plugin.debug
name: DebugPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

Developer debugging utilities for `DXOS` Composer — a debug panel (console, log viewer, and every developer tool as a navtree) docked as the deck's bottom drawer or floated as a window, synthetic-object generation, a per-object Debug companion, a space-objects browser, a wireframe overlay, and a transient-stats panel. The panel is a navtree over a hidden `root/debug` app-graph category that this plugin contributes; the Devtools inspector (`org.dxos.plugin.devtools`) attaches its tree there too. Nothing under `root/debug` appears in the main navtree, opens in the deck, or has a URL.

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document.

| Term        | URI                            |
|-------------|--------------------------------|
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`        |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`        |
| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0`   |
| `test`      | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`        |
| `decision`  | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0`    |

## Types

```mdl
type Settings
  fields:
    wireframe?: boolean         # overlay every article/section surface with a labelled border
    traceAll?: boolean          # enable verbose operation tracing across all plugins
```

```mdl
type DebugPluginOptions
  fields:
    logStore?: IdbLogStore      # shared persistent log store for capturing and downloading logs
    persistStats?: boolean      # persist the stats-panel store to localStorage (default true)
```

```mdl
type DebugPanelMode
  literals: docked | floating   # docked = the deck's bottom drawer (default); floating = a FloatingPanel window
```

```mdl
type DebugPanelViewState
  desc: Persisted (localStorage) so a debugging session survives the reloads it provokes; the floating window's open/closed state is not part of it (the drawer's is deck state).
  fields:
    nodeId?: string             # qualified id of the selected page (e.g. root/debug/devtools/client/config)
    open: string[]              # path keys of the expanded branches
    mode?: DebugPanelMode       # which host shows the panel; absent reads as docked
    position?: Point            # where the floating panel was last dragged
    size?: Size                 # the size the floating panel was last resized to
```

## App graph

```mdl
feat DebugCategory
  desc: |
    A hidden `debug` category on the app-graph root: node `root/debug`, type
    `GraphPath.GroupTypes.debug`, `data: null`, `disposition: hidden`, not draggable or droppable.
    `hidden` is what the main navtree already filters, so no consumer has to learn about the
    category. Tool plugins attach with `AppNodeMatcher.whenDebugGroup`; every tool is a global node
    (no per-space enumeration) and a space-scoped tool resolves the active workspace's space at
    render time. Nothing under `root/debug` declares a URL binding.
```

| Node                        | Type                | Article                    |
|-----------------------------|---------------------|----------------------------|
| `root/debug/debug`          | `DebugNodes.id`     | branch (no article); open with the console selected on a pristine panel |
| `root/debug/debug/console`  | `DebugNodes.Console`| Console (agent debug shell)|
| `root/debug/debug/logs`     | `DebugNodes.Logs`   | Log viewer                 |
| `root/debug/debug/space`    | `DebugNodes.SpaceType` | Generate objects (active space) |
| `root/debug/devtools/...`   | contributed by `org.dxos.plugin.devtools` | Client / HALO / ECHO / MESH / EDGE inspectors, App Graph, SDK Explorer, CLI |

## Components

```mdl
component DebugPanel
  desc: |
    The debug surface in parts (`DebugPanel.Root` / `Body` / `Sidebar` / `Main`) with two hosts
    that share one `Root` context, so selection and open branches carry over when the panel moves:
    `DebugPanelDrawer` renders it in the deck's bottom drawer (`AppSurface.Drawer`,
    `org.dxos.role.drawer`) and `DebugPanelStatus` hosts it in a `FloatingPanel`. `Root` owns the
    selection, the expanded branches and the mode (DebugPanelViewState); `Body` is the `Splitter`
    over `Sidebar` — a `Tree` over `root/debug` via `useGraphTreeModel`
    (`@dxos/plugin-graph/hooks`) — and `Main`, the selected node's article surface.
    `DebugPanelHeader` is the title-bar control both hosts share: a dock/float switch
    (`debugPanel.mode`) and, for the drawer, a close (`debugPanel.close`). Selecting a row never
    touches the deck, attention, or the URL; the drawer is not a plank and never takes attention.
  props:
    contextId?: string          # view-state context; defaults to the status-rail singleton
  state:
    nodeId?: string             # selected page
    open: string[]              # expanded branch path keys
    mode: DebugPanelMode        # docked (default) or floating
  actions:
    select(nodeId: string)      # a leaf shows its article; a branch (data: null) toggles instead
    setOpen(pathKey: string, isOpen: boolean)
    setMode(mode: DebugPanelMode)  # float: close the drawer, open the window; dock: close the window, open the drawer
  layout: |
    ┌─ Main.Drawer (docked, default) ──────────────────────┐
    │ Debug                                          ⇱ ×  │
    ├─ Sidebar (Tree) ──┬─ Main (article surface) ────────┤
    │ ▾ Debug           │                                  │
    │   ▸ Console       │  Config JSON / log table /       │
    │     Logs          │  generator / ...                 │
    │     Generate obj. │                                  │
    │ ▾ DevTools        │  (console and logs stay mounted  │
    │   ▾ Client        │   while another page is shown)   │
    │       Config      │                                  │
    └───────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘
    Floating: the same Sidebar/Main under a FloatingPanel title bar (⇲ dock, — minimize, ×).
```

## Features

```mdl
feat DebugPanel
  desc: |
    A status-bar button shows the debug panel: docked (the default) it toggles the deck's bottom
    drawer via `LayoutOperation.UpdateDrawer`, so the planks reflow above it, its height is dragged
    from the top edge and persists with the deck's layout, and fullscreen hides it; floating, it
    opens the panel as a window over the app. The title bar's dock/float control moves the panel
    between the two with the same tool selected. The sidebar lists Debug (Console, Logs, Generate
    objects — a pristine panel opens Debug with the console selected) and DevTools, and the
    selection, open branches, and mode persist across reloads.
```

```mdl
feat GenerateObjects
  desc: Create configurable batches of synthetic ECHO objects into the active workspace's space, from the Debug → Generate objects page of the panel.
```

```mdl
feat ObjectDebugCompanion
  desc: A Debug companion tab on every ECHO object exposing raw field values and DXNs.
```

```mdl
feat SpaceObjectsBrowser
  desc: A deck companion listing all objects in the active space with live reactive updates.
```

```mdl
feat Wireframe
  desc: Overlay labelled borders around every article and section surface.
```

```mdl
feat StatsPanel
  desc: |
    The shared telemetry store (`AppCapabilities.StatsPanel`) as one `StatCard` per plugin
    compartment. Rendered on the plugin's own `org.dxos.plugin.debug.surface.stats` role (with an
    empty-state card) and contributed to `AppSurface.DevtoolsOverview` at position 20 (no card when
    the store is empty), so it stacks under the devtools cards in the Stats companion.
```

## QA

Executable tests. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps to an
invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by test and step number.

plugin-debug is a dev-only utility plugin: it defaults on in `isDev` builds (see
`packages/apps/composer-app/src/plugin-defs.tsx`), off otherwise.

```mdl
test QA-1: Insert Lorem ipsum from the slash menu
  desc: The one debug feature with a real operation behind it — the MenuExtension contribution used as the worked example in plugin-markdown's QA-3.
  actors: both
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open and contains at least one collection
    - plugin-debug is enabled (dev-only default; see above)
    - attendableId: an empty document is open in the editor, its attendable id
  steps:
    - name: Open the command menu
      do: With the caret on an empty line, type `/`.
      expect: the popup lists a "Debug" group containing "Lorem ipsum", below the built-in groups
      note: |
        NO `invoke` — opening the popup is a real keystroke and CodeMirror does not observe
        synthetic input events. Human-only step; an agent run reports it unverified.
    - name: Insert a paragraph
      do: Select "Lorem ipsum".
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.debug.insertLoremIpsum { subject: $given.attendableId, head: 0 }
      expect: a paragraph of placeholder prose appears at the caret
      assert: return view.state.doc.length > 0;
  after:
    - name: Clear the document
      do: Select all and delete.
      expect: the editor is empty and the document body is an empty string
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Generate synthetic objects into a collection
  desc: The SpaceGenerator UI (GenerateObjects) — no operation exists behind it, so this is a human-only sanity check that the debug tooling itself works.
  actors: human
  covers: [GenerateObjects, SpaceObjectsBrowser]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - collection: a collection in that space, empty of synthetic objects
    - plugin-debug is enabled (dev-only default; see above)
  steps:
    - name: Open the debug panel and generate objects
      do: |
        Click the status-bar debug button (terminal icon), then in the panel's sidebar open
        Debug → Generate objects. Set the batch size (e.g. 5) and press `+` on
        `org.dxos.type.document`.
      expect: |
        the type's count rises by 5, the documents appear in the space-objects browser, and the
        main navtree's SYSTEM group still shows only Database (no DevTools or Debug node)
      note: NO `invoke` — GenerateObjects has no operation; it is a UI-only synthetic-data generator.
  after:
    - name: Remove the generated objects
      do: Select the generated documents in the space-objects browser and delete them.
      expect: the collection returns to its prior state
```

```mdl
test QA-3: Walk the review-queue demo space
  desc: |
    The demo script for the Review template (`samples/review`): seed a space holding one project whose
    finished tasks carry merged dxos/dxos pull requests with their screenshots and demo recordings,
    and whose tasks in review carry the PRs still open — then walk it the way a reviewer would.
  actors: both
  status: passing  # 2026-09-24, agent run recorded as a demo video
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - defaultSpace: the default space, which is where the run returns to
    - plugin-debug, plugin-projects, plugin-tasks, plugin-file and plugin-video are enabled (dev-only defaults)
    - the browser can reach raw.githubusercontent.com, which serves the screenshots
  before:
    - name: Seed the space
      do: Create a space from the "Composer — Review queue" template.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.debug.createSpaceFromTemplate { id: "org.dxos.plugin-debug.template.review" }
      capture: seeded
      expect: a space named "Composer — Review queue" appears in the space list
      assert: return $snapshot.spaces.some((s) => s.id === $result.spaceId)
      note: |
        The template fetches four PNGs while it applies, so on a cold server the step takes up to a minute.
        A screenshot the browser cannot reach is kept as an external blob naming its URL — this step still
        passes, and the image step below is where that shows.
    - name: Find the project
      do: Open the space and expand Projects in the navtree.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.queryObjects { typename: "org.dxos.type.project" }
      space: $seeded.spaceId
      capture: projects
      expect: Projects lists one project, "Composer polish"
      assert: return $result.results.length === 1 && $result.results[0].label === 'Composer polish'
  steps:
    - name: Open the project's tasks
      do: Click "Composer polish", then its Tasks tab.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.open { subject: ["root/$seeded.spaceId/ai/org.dxos.type.project/$projects.results[0].dxn.objectId"] }
      expect: |
        seven tasks: three done, each with a violet merged `#` pill and image or video tags; two in review,
        each with a green open `#` pill and a video tag; one started; one to do
      assert: |
        const tasks = await dxos.spaces($seeded.spaceId).db.query(dxos.Filter.type(dxos.DXN.make('org.dxos.type.task'))).run();
        const count = (status) => tasks.filter((task) => task.status === status).length;
        const opened = $snapshot.planks.some((plank) => plank.subject?.dxn === $projects.results[0].dxn);
        return opened && tasks.length === 7 && count('done') === 3 && count('review') === 2;
      note: The Tasks tab is a view switch inside the plank, so the human clicks it; the agent judges from the database.
    - name: Look at a finished task's screenshot
      do: On "Run desktop OAuth in the system browser", click the `oauth-pending.png` tag.
      expect: a preview card shows the screenshot of the sign-in form waiting on the provider
      assert: |
        const blobs = await dxos.spaces($seeded.spaceId).db.query(dxos.Filter.type(dxos.DXN.make('org.dxos.type.blob'))).run();
        return blobs.length === 4 && blobs.every((blob) => blob.data._tag === 'inline' && blob.size > 0);
      note: NO `invoke` — the tag is a pointer gesture on the task row, with no operation behind it.
    - name: Review an open pull request
      do: On "Switch identities in place instead of a reset and reload" (in review), click the `#13336` pill.
      expect: the preview names dxos/dxos#13336, state open, by dmaretskyi
      assert: |
        const prs = await dxos.spaces($seeded.spaceId).db.query(dxos.Filter.type(dxos.DXN.make('org.dxos.type.pullRequest'))).run();
        return prs.filter((pr) => pr.state === 'open').map((pr) => pr.number).sort().join() === '12952,13336';
      note: NO `invoke` — as above.
    - name: Watch a finished task's demo
      do: On "Count down a toast's remaining time", click the "Toast countdown demo" tag, then Open from its ⋮ menu.
      expect: |
        the video opens in the project's plank (breadcrumb "Composer polish » Toast countdown demo") and plays
        the toast counting down; the Transcript tab says transcripts are for YouTube videos only, and no error
        toast appears
      assert: |
        return $snapshot.planks.some((plank) => plank.subject?.typename === 'org.dxos.type.video') &&
          !$snapshot.toasts.some((toast) => toast.title === 'Transcription failed.') && $stepErrors.length === 0;
      note: NO `invoke` — as above. The recording streams from its URL into the player; nothing is written.
  after:
    - name: Delete the space
      do: Open the space's settings and delete the space.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.delete { space: Space($seeded.spaceId) }
      expect: the space leaves the space list
      assert: return !$snapshot.spaces.some((s) => s.id === $seeded.spaceId)
    - name: Return to the default space
      do: Pick the default space in the space list.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.switchWorkspace { subject: "root/$given.defaultSpace.id" }
      expect: the workspace is the default space and no error is logged
      assert: return $snapshot.layout.workspace === `root/${$given.defaultSpace.id}` && $stepErrors.length === 0
```

```mdl
test QA-4: Alien Labs — a customer asks for one more thing
  desc: |
    The first Alien Labs vignette, a working moment for Astrid Holm, engineering manager. Okapi had a
    cache-corruption incident, and their engineering director has replied to the postmortem with one
    more request. Astrid reads it, checks who is asking, and finds the follow-up meeting already on
    the calendar. Recorded by autocue/fieldstone-customer-followup.mjs.
  actors: human
  status: passing           # 2026-10-16, recorded by autocue/fieldstone-customer-followup.mjs
  given:
    - an identity exists, on a fresh profile
    - plugin-debug is enabled (dev-only default)
    - space: a space created from the "Alien Labs" template, open, with the navtree expanded
  steps:
    - name: Look at the week
      do: Open the Alien Labs calendar, scroll the month grid a little ahead and back, then pick Monday 12 October, Wednesday 21 October and Wednesday 14 October in turn.
      expect: days ringed for their events; each pick moves the event list to that day, with the Okapi follow-up on the 21st
      note: The grid's highlighted "today" is the wall clock's, not the template's reference date.
    - name: Open an event
      do: Open one of the 14 October events from the list.
      expect: the event with its notes and up to three attendees, then "+N more"
    - name: Find the thread
      do: Open the Inbox and scroll back to "Okapi cache-corruption postmortem".
      expect: the thread, five weeks old, with eight messages
    - name: Open the conversation
      do: Open the thread.
      expect: the conversation view lists its messages, with the thread summary
    - name: Read Nadia's reply
      do: Open Nadia Petrov's message, "Read it twice. Clear and honest, thank you."
      expect: her message expands; she asks for the hash-mismatch alert to reach Okapi too
    - name: Check who is asking
      do: Hover Nadia's avatar on that message.
      expect: her card shows Okapi Payments, her email, recent messages and the upcoming follow-up
    - name: Open her contact
      do: Open her contact from the card's menu.
      expect: her contact opens, with her related messages and events
    - name: Find the follow-up
      do: Open "Okapi alerting follow-up" from her upcoming events.
      expect: the follow-up a week out, with Nadia, Sam, Omar and Astrid, on the Alien Labs calendar
```

```mdl
test QA-5: Alien Labs — what the agents did this week
  desc: |
    The second Alien Labs vignette. Astrid checks on the Cairn 1.8 release work, most of which agents
    do: the project's Gantt shows each Builder and Reviewer run, one run's chat shows the work, and the
    failed R2 task shows where a person stepped in. Recorded by autocue/fieldstone-agents-week.mjs.
  actors: human
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists, on a fresh profile
    - plugin-debug is enabled (dev-only default)
    - space: a space created from the "Alien Labs" template, open, with Projects expanded
  steps:
    - name: Open the release project
      do: Open "Cairn 1.8: remote cache v2" and its Tasks tab.
      expect: twenty tasks, most assigned to agents, with done, review, started, blocked and failed ones
    - name: See the runs on the Gantt
      do: Open the pipeline.
      expect: one lane per Builder and Reviewer run, overlapping across the week, and bars for the tasks they worked
    - name: Look inside a run
      do: Expand the project's Sessions in the navtree and open "Builder: Fix cache-key collision on concurrent writes".
      expect: the brief, the agent's steps with their tool calls (Read file, Run tests, Open pull request, Update tasks) and its closing summary
    - name: Find where a person stepped in
      do: Open "Integration tests against R2".
      expect: failed after a run and a retry, with Omar's note in its history; "Switch beta tenants to the R2 backend" is blocked on it
```

```mdl
test QA-6: Alien Labs — the customer pipeline
  desc: |
    The third Alien Labs vignette, Alien Labs' accounts as structured data: the CRM section's
    organizations and people, the pricing sheet behind a quote, and a customer note whose links lead to
    its contacts. Recorded by autocue/fieldstone-customer-pipeline.mjs.
